---
import ComparisonPage from '../../../components/ComparisonPage.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../utils/paths';
import {
  APP_STORE_SEARCH_API_URL,
  COMPARISONS_CHECKED_ON,
  TRACER_ADVANTAGES,
  TRACER_RIVALS,
  TRACER_TABLE,
  listingUrl,
} from '../../../data/comparisons';
import { TRACER_COMPARE_CALLS } from '../../../data/storeCalls';

const GOLF_TRACER_URL: string = listingUrl(TRACER_RIVALS.golfTracer);
---
<ComparisonPage
  pageTitle="Golf Ball Tracer Apps Compared for iPhone | SundayStroke"
  description="SundayStroke Tracer next to four iPhone golf ball tracer apps: disc golf support, automatic tracking you can correct, handheld filming and what it costs to start. Every fact sourced and dated."
  heading="Golf ball tracer apps compared"
  intro="Five iPhone apps that put a flight line on a golf video: ours, SundayStroke Tracer, and four others on the App Store. This page sets out where Tracer comes out ahead, with every competitor fact linked to the listing it came from."
  advantages={TRACER_ADVANTAGES}
  table={TRACER_TABLE}
  checkedOn={COMPARISONS_CHECKED_ON}
  methodSourceUrl={APP_STORE_SEARCH_API_URL}
  calls={TRACER_COMPARE_CALLS}
>
  <h2>One app for golf and disc golf</h2>
  <p>
    Tracer opens on two modes. <strong>Trace golf</strong> is for golf balls: you put the cursor on the ball at the start of the
    flight and Tracer follows it through the air. <strong>Free curve</strong> is for everything else: drives and putts with a disc,
    a chip you want to draw by hand, any flight at all. In Free curve, Tracer follows a disc on its own once you select it at
    release, and you add or move key points wherever the line drifts.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you play both games, or you film a disc golf card on Saturday and a golf round on Sunday, you only need one app. Our <a href={sitePath('/tracer/disc-golf/')}>disc golf page</a>
    shows a real traced throw, and the <a href={sitePath('/learn/film-a-disc-flight/')}>disc filming tutorial</a> walks
    you through the settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Automatic tracking you can correct</h2>
  <p>
    Automatic tracking saves the slow part, but review the result: something else in the frame can pull a line off the ball. Tracer
    lets you correct it instead of starting again:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Move the landing point and the arc</strong> by hand in Trace golf, so the line ends where the ball came down.</li>
    <li><strong>Trim the track</strong> in the timeline to leave out frames where tracking picked up something else.</li>
    <li><strong>Add or move key points</strong> in Free curve where a disc line drifts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tracer gives you the automatic pass and the manual fixes in the free download. The Golf Tracer's listing describes a manual tracer
    (<a href={GOLF_TRACER_URL}>The Golf Tracer listing</a>). Checked on {COMPARISONS_CHECKED_ON}. When a line goes wrong, the
    <a href={sitePath('/learn/fix-a-trace-by-hand/')}>fix a trace by hand tutorial</a> shows each correction.
  </p>

  <h2>Film handheld, no tripod</h2>
  <p>
    No tripod is required: Tracer keeps the line in place when the camera moves, so you can film with the phone in your hands or a
    friend's.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keeping the footage reasonably steady and the ball visible still makes the flight easier to trace. The
    <a href={sitePath('/learn/film-a-golf-ball-flight/')}>golf filming tutorial</a> covers where to stand and how wide to frame.
  </p>

  <h2>Make the line look the way you want</h2>
  <p>Once the flight is traced, you choose how it looks:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A single color or a gradient, with the width you like and a 3D look.</li>
    <li>Effects such as electricity and wave, a tail length, and landing effects.</li>
    <li>Color segments: with the clear style, a section of the line turns transparent, to show the flight behind an obstacle.</li>
    <li>A distance label: you type the distance and a counter runs up during the flight. Tracer does not measure distance, so use a number you measured separately.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When you're happy, save the video to Photos and share it wherever you post your clips.</p>

  <h2>What you get without paying</h2>
  <p>
    Tracer is a free download. The free version includes automatic ball tracking, Free curve, the Floodlit and white lines with
    width and 3D look, and some free saves with a small watermark. Premium adds unlimited saves with no watermark, more line
    styles, colors and gradients, color segments, the distance label and the effects.
  </p>
  <p>With Tracer you can trace your own clips first and decide on Premium after you've seen the result.</p>

  <h2>Tracer is a good fit if you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>play disc golf, or both games, and want one app for every flight;</li>
    <li>film handheld and don't want to carry a tripod;</li>
    <li>want automatic ball tracking, with manual fixes when the line slips;</li>
    <li>want to try automatic tracking on your own videos before you pay anything.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions golfers ask</h2>
  <h3>Does automatic tracking mean no editing?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Watch the result before you share it. If the line strays, move the landing point and
    arc, or trim the frames where tracking picked up something else.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I trace a disc golf putt?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Use Free curve. Select the disc at release and Tracer follows it, or place the points by hand for a short putt.</p>
  <h3>Do I need a tripod?</h3>
  <p>No. The line stays in place when the camera moves. Reasonably steady footage still makes tracing easier.</p>
  <h3>Does Tracer know how far the ball went?</h3>
  <p>No. You type the distance and Tracer shows it as a counter that runs up during the flight.</p>
  <h3>Is Tracer free?</h3>
  <p>Tracer is free to download and try, with some free saves that carry a small watermark. Premium removes the watermark and adds styles and effects.</p>

  <p>
    See everything Tracer does on the <a href={sitePath('/tracer/')}>Tracer page</a>, or compare
    <a href={sitePath('/compare/golf-gps/')}>golf GPS apps</a> for the course itself.
  </p>
</ComparisonPage>
